'''''Salvia confertiflora''''', the '''Sabra spike sage''',<ref name = RHSPF/> is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[family (botany)|family]] Lamiaceae, [[native species|native]] to Brazil. This [[herbaceous]] [[perennial plant|perennial]] reaches {{cvt|1-1.5|m|ft|abbr=on}} in height, and {{cvt|10-50|cm|in|abbr=on}} spread in one season, becoming shrubby at the base with age.<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/16324/Salvia-confertiflora/Details | title = ''Salvia confertiflora'' | website = www.rhs.org | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 17 September 2020}}</ref>

The dark green leaves, with a yellow undertone, are about {{convert|7|in|cm|abbr=on}} long by {{convert|3.5|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, with [[Leaf#Margins .28edge.29|serrated edges]], and with velvety red-brown hairs on the [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]] and stem of the new leaves. The leaves bear a sage scent. The [[inflorescence]]s are covered with velvety red-brown hairs, with the stems and the [[Calyx (botany)|calyx]] also having a red-brown color. The {{cvt|0.5|in|cm|abbr=on}} flowers are orange-red, and very profuse, explaining the epithet ''confertiflora'', or "crowded with flowers". The plant grows so large that it needs staking and protection from wind in gardens.<ref name="Clebsch">{{cite book|last1=Clebsch|first1=Betsy|last2=Barner|first2=Carol D.|title=The New Book of Salvias|publisher=Timber Press|year=2003|page=86|isbn=978-0-88192-560-9|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NM0iwB8GrQYC&pg=PA86}}</ref>

The plant is cultivated as an ornamental in temperate and tropical horticulture. As it does not tolerate freezing temperatures, it requires protection in cold temperate zones, and a sheltered position in full sun.<ref name = RHSPF/>

